<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Aragats</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aragats</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Aragats is the highest mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, an extinct volcano rising to 4,090 metres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>. Its slopes have been settled since prehistoric times and carry the medieval fortress of Amberd and the Byurakan Astrophysical Observatory. In the 9th and 8th centuries BCE the lands below it were part of Etiuni, which the kings of Urartu repeatedly attacked.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-aragats.jpg" alt="Mount Aragats" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Armenak Margarian · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Aragats_2020-05-11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aragats is an isolated volcanic massif with four summits, standing about 40 kilometres northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>. Its northern summit, at 4,090 metres, is the highest point of the Lesser Caucasus and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. The other peaks are the western at 3,995 metres, the eastern at 3,908 and the southern at 3,888. The massif is bounded by the Kasagh River on the east, the Akhurian on the west, the Ararat Plain on the south and the Shirak Plain on the north. It is about 200 kilometres around and covers some 6,000 square kilometres, around a fifth of the area of Armenia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The volcano was built in four phases, from a first phase possibly 2.5 million years ago to a fourth between 0.56 and 0.45 million years ago, and its lava flows range in age from the middle Pleistocene to 3,000 BCE. Rock paintings in the Kasagh River valley may date to the early Holocene, and petroglyphs showing animals at Aghavnatun were possibly made between the 4th and 1st millennia BCE. According to Armenian tradition the name means Ara's throne, after the hero Ara the Handsome, though this is almost certainly a folk etymology. The early medieval historian Movses Khorenatsi says the mountain was named after Aramaneak, son of the patriarch Hayk.</p><p>A later name, Alagöz, meaning variegated eye in Turkish and Azerbaijani, was widely used until the mid-20th century. Legend tells that Aragats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a> were sisters who parted after a quarrel, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a>, who converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the early 4th century, prayed on the peak by the light of a lamp hanging from heaven without a rope. Both Aragats and Ararat are held sacred by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. A 4th-century mausoleum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a> stands at the village of Aghtsk, and the fortress of Amberd and the 11th-century Vahramashen Church lie on the slopes at 2,300 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Glaciers in the crater, which covered 5.5 to 5.8 square kilometres in 1896, have retreated since. The Aragats Cosmic Ray Research Station, near Lake Kari at about 3,200 metres, was founded in 1943 by the brothers Artem Alikhanian and Abram Alikhanov. The Byurakan Observatory, established in 1946 by Victor Ambartsumian on the southern slopes, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> one of the world's centres of astrophysics in the 20th century. The settlements on the slopes include Ashtarak, Aparan and Talin, and the mountain appears on the coat of arms of Gyumri.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Aragats” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 9th century BCE the name Etiuni covered a cluster of tribal lands north of the Araxes, around Lake Sevan and the plain beneath <b>Mount Aragats</b>, which the expanding Kingdom of Urartu treated as one hostile country.</blockquote><a href="/state/etiuni">Etiuni</a></li></ul></section>
